Subject: [flux] Make the console column and the avatar clickable

The account avatar opened nothing when clicked, and the sidebar's search and
collapse buttons did nothing either. Neither was a bug in their handlers -
the click never reached them.

Both views are children of BrowserView placed in the band along the top and
left that BrowserView::NonClientHitTest treats as titlebar. On Windows that
band answers WM_NCHITTEST with HTCAPTION, at which point the system starts a
window drag and never delivers a client mouse event at all. The button's
PressedCallback is wired correctly and simply never runs.

Claimed before the web-app toolbar check, following the same shape upstream
uses for views that live in the caption area. The whole of each view is client
area deliberately: the sidebar is a WebView whose every pixel is interactive,
and the avatar is a button. The tab strip beside them still drags the window.

--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/frame/browser_view.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/frame/browser_view.cc
@@ -4591,6 +4591,32 @@
   views::View::ConvertPointToTarget(parent(), this,
                                     &point_in_browser_view_coords);
 
+  // Flux's own frame furniture claims its hits before anything below can call
+  // the area caption.
+  //
+  // The console column and the account avatar are children of the browser
+  // view that occupy the band this function otherwise treats as titlebar. On
+  // Windows that band answers WM_NCHITTEST with HTCAPTION, so the system
+  // begins a window drag and NO client mouse event is ever delivered - the
+  // avatar's menu never opened and the sidebar's own buttons did nothing, for
+  // a reason no amount of reading their click handlers could reveal.
+  //
+  // The whole of each view is client area on purpose: the sidebar is a WebView
+  // and every pixel of it is interactive, and the avatar is a button. The tab
+  // strip to their right is still there to drag the window by.
+  if (flux_sidebar_ && flux_sidebar_->GetVisible()) {
+    gfx::Point test_point(point);
+    if (ConvertedHitTest(parent(), flux_sidebar_, &test_point)) {
+      return HTCLIENT;
+    }
+  }
+  if (flux_avatar_ && flux_avatar_->GetVisible()) {
+    gfx::Point test_point(point);
+    if (ConvertedHitTest(parent(), flux_avatar_, &test_point)) {
+      return HTCLIENT;
+    }
+  }
+
   // Check if the point is in the web_app_frame_toolbar_. Because this toolbar
   // can entirely be within the window controls overlay area, this check needs
   // to be done before the window controls overlay area check below.
